IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-cpc-interface`
IMPORT `ea-types`

-- Evidence Act 1893: presumptions (section 4, sections 81 to 92, 114, 116 and 116A) and the burden of
-- proof (sections 103 to 113).
-- Source: source/EA1893.txt lines 577 to 589, 2724 to 2870, 3104 to 3460.
--
-- The questions this module answers: DOES THE COURT PRESUME A FACT, AND HOW STRONGLY? and ON WHOM DOES
-- THE BURDEN OF PROVING A FACT LIE?  Section 4 gives the three strengths: "may presume", "is to
-- presume" and "conclusive proof".  Each presumption in the Act is encoded as the strength it carries,
-- or no answer where its conditions are not met.
--
-- In a prosecution the burden rules are the ones that put a matter on the accused:
-- section 107 (the Penal Code's general exceptions, and any special exception or proviso, are for the
-- accused, and the court presumes their absence) and section 108 (facts especially within a person's
-- knowledge).  Section 103 and illustration (a) put on the prosecution the burden of proving that the
-- accused committed the crime.

§ `4. The three kinds of presumption`

-- 4(1): where the Act provides that the court MAY presume a fact, it may regard the fact as proved unless
-- and until it is disproved, or may call for proof of it.
-- 4(2): where the Act directs that the court IS TO presume a fact, it is to regard the fact as proved
-- unless and until it is disproved.
-- 4(3): when one fact is declared CONCLUSIVE PROOF of another, the court is, on proof of the one fact, to
-- regard the other as proved and is not to allow evidence to be given to disprove it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 4(1), (2) and (3)"
GIVEN k IS A `Kind of Presumption`, `the fact has been disproved` IS A BOOLEAN, `the court calls for proof` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Treatment of a Presumed Fact`
`the treatment of a presumed fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `the court may presume` THEN
        BRANCH IF `the court calls for proof` THEN `proof of it is called for`
               IF `the fact has been disproved` THEN `not regarded as proved`
               OTHERWISE `regarded as proved`
    WHEN `the court is to presume` THEN
        BRANCH IF `the fact has been disproved` THEN `not regarded as proved`
               OTHERWISE `regarded as proved`
    WHEN `conclusive proof` THEN `regarded as proved`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 4(3)"
GIVEN k IS A `Kind of Presumption`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence may be given to disprove the presumed fact` IF
    NOT k EQUALS `conclusive proof`

§ `81 to 92. Presumptions as to documents`

-- Each rule returns the strength of the presumption when its conditions are met, and NOTHING when they are
-- not (the Act then neither presumes nor forbids proof).  "Genuine" and the other things presumed are
-- stated in the comment on each.

-- 81(1): the court is to presume genuine every document purporting to be a certificate, certified copy or
-- other document declared by law admissible as evidence of a particular fact, purporting to be duly
-- certified by a public officer in Singapore or an authorised officer in Malaysia, if substantially in the
-- form and purporting to be executed in the manner directed by law.  81(2): the court is also to presume
-- that the officer signing held the official character claimed.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 81(1) and (2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 81` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     p's `the document purports to be a certificate, certified copy or other document declared by law admissible as evidence of a particular fact`
              AND p's `it purports to be duly certified by a public officer in Singapore or by an authorised officer in Malaysia`
              AND p's `it is substantially in the form and purports to be executed in the manner directed by law`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 82: the court is to presume (a) that the document is genuine, (b) that statements as to the circumstances
-- under which it was taken, purporting to be made by the person signing it, are true, and (c) that the
-- evidence, statement or confession was duly taken.  The document is a record of witness evidence, or a
-- statement or confession by a prisoner or accused person taken in accordance with law, purporting to be
-- signed by a Judge or Magistrate or authorised officer.  It bears on the confession recorded under CPC
-- section 22, 23 or 280, but does not decide whether that statement is admissible (CPC s 258).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 82(a), (b) and (c)"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 82` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     (   p's `the document purports to be a record or memorandum of the evidence, or of part of the evidence, given by a witness in a judicial proceeding or before an officer authorised by law to take such evidence`
                   OR p's `the document purports to be a statement or confession by a prisoner or accused person, taken in accordance with law`)
              AND p's `the document purports to be signed by a Judge or Magistrate or by an authorised officer`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 83: the court is to presume the genuineness of every document purporting to be a Gazette or a newspaper or
-- journal, and of every document purporting to be a document directed by law to be kept by any person, if
-- kept substantially in the form required by law and produced from proper custody.  The "if" is read to
-- attach to the second class only: see NOTES.md, fork F6.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 83"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 83` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the document purports to be the Government Gazette of Singapore or of Malaysia or of a part of the Commonwealth, a Gazette issued by the local government of a part of Malaysia or the Commonwealth, or a newspaper or journal`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           IF     p's `the document purports to be a document directed by law to be kept by a person`
              AND p's `it is kept substantially in the form required by law`
              AND p's `it is produced from proper custody`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 84: the court is to presume that the seal, stamp or signature is genuine and the person signing held the
-- official character claimed; and the document is admissible for the same purpose as in England or
-- Northern Ireland.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 84(a) and (b)"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 84` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the document purports to be one that by the law in force in England or Northern Ireland would be admissible in proof of a particular in a court there without proof of the seal, stamp, signature or official character`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 85(1): the court is to presume that maps or plans purporting to be made by the authority of the Government
-- were so made and are accurate.  85(2): maps or plans made for the purposes of a cause or proceeding, civil or
-- criminal, must be proved to be accurate.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 85(1)"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 85(1)` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the map or plan purports to be made by the authority of the Government`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 85(2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accuracy of the map or plan must be proved` IF
    p's `the map or plan is made for the purposes of a cause or other proceeding, civil or criminal`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 86(a) and (b)"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 86` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     p's `the publication purports to be printed or published under the authority of the government of a country and to contain a law of that country`
              OR  p's `the publication purports to contain an official or authoritative report of a decision of a court of a country`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 87"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 87` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the document purports to be a power of attorney executed before and authenticated by a notary public or a court, Judge, Magistrate or consular officer of Singapore`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 88"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 88` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the document purports to be a certified copy of a judicial record of a country not forming part of the Commonwealth, certified in the manner commonly in use in that country as certified by a representative of the President or of Her Britannic Majesty in or for that country`
           THEN JUST `the court may presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 89"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 89` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the book is one to which the court may refer for information on matters of public or general interest, or the map or chart is published and its statements are relevant facts`
           THEN JUST `the court may presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 90: the court may presume that a message forwarded from a telegraph office to the addressee corresponds with
-- a message delivered for transmission; the court is not to make any presumption as to the person by whom
-- the message was delivered for transmission.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 90"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 90` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the message was forwarded from a telegraph office to the person to whom it purports to be addressed`
           THEN JUST `the court may presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 91"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 91` MEANS
    BRANCH IF p's `the document was called for after notice to produce given under section 68 and was not produced`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 92: where a document purporting or proved to be 30 years old is produced from custody the court in the
-- particular case considers proper, the court may presume that the signature and every other part purporting
-- to be in a person's handwriting is in that person's handwriting and, in the case of a document executed or
-- attested, that it was duly executed and attested.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 92 and Explanation"
GIVEN p IS A `Presumption Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 92` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     p's `the document purports or is proved to be 30 years old`
              AND p's `it is produced from a custody the court in the particular case considers proper`
           THEN JUST `the court may presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `114. Rebuttable presumption of paternity`

-- 114(1): where a person was born during a valid marriage between the mother and a man, or within 280 days
-- after its dissolution, the mother remaining unmarried, it is presumed that the person is the legitimate
-- child of that man, unless the contrary is proved.  114(2): not for a person whose parenthood is determined
-- under the Status of Children (Assisted Reproduction Technology) Act 2013.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 114(1) and (2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Paternity Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is presumed to be the legitimate child of the man` IF
        (   p's `the person was born during the continuance of a valid marriage between the person's mother and a man`
         OR p's `the person was born within 280 days after the dissolution of the marriage, the mother remaining unmarried`)
    AND NOT p's `the person's parenthood is determined under the Status of Children (Assisted Reproduction Technology) Act 2013`

§ `116. Presumptions of fact`

-- 116: the court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being
-- had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business, in their
-- relation to the facts of the particular case.  The nine illustrations (including (a), possession of stolen
-- goods soon after the theft, and (b), the caution due to an accomplice's evidence) are examples of what the
-- court may presume, and each is qualified by the facts the court must also regard; they add no condition
-- and are inert here.  The Criminal Procedure Code makes the presumption about stolen property specific to
-- proof of knowledge in sections 265 and 266.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 116 and illustrations (a) to (i)"
GIVEN `the court thinks the fact likely to have happened, having regard to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business, in their relation to the facts of the particular case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may presume the fact under section 116` IF
    `the court thinks the fact likely to have happened, having regard to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business, in their relation to the facts of the particular case`

§ `116A. Presumptions in relation to electronic records`

-- 116A(1): unless evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption is adduced, where a device or
-- process is one that, or is of a kind that, if properly used, ordinarily produces or accurately communicates
-- an electronic record, the court is to presume that in producing or communicating the record on the occasion
-- in question the device or process produced or accurately communicated it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 116A(1)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Electronic Record Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 116A(1)` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     e's `the device or process is one that, or is of a kind that, if properly used, ordinarily produces or accurately communicates an electronic record`
              AND NOT e's `evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption is adduced`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 116A(4): for 116A(2), in criminal proceedings a party to the proceedings includes the police officer or
-- other officer of a law enforcement agency who was involved in the investigation of the offences allegedly
-- committed by the accused person, and an accomplice of the accused person even if not charged in the same
-- proceedings.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 116A(2) and (4)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Electronic Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the generator of the electronic record is a non-party for section 116A(2)` IF
        e's `the electronic record was generated, recorded or stored in the usual and ordinary course of business by a person who was not a party to the proceedings on the occasion in question`
    AND NOT (   e's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
            AND (   e's `the person who generated, recorded or stored it was a police officer or other officer of a law enforcement agency involved in the investigation of the offences allegedly committed by the accused person`
                 OR e's `the person who generated, recorded or stored it was an accomplice of the accused person, whether or not charged in the same proceedings`))

-- 116A(2): unless evidence to the contrary is adduced, the court is to presume that an electronic record
-- generated, recorded or stored is authentic if it is established that it was generated, recorded or stored in
-- the usual and ordinary course of business by a person who was not a party and who did not generate, record
-- or store it under the control of the party seeking to introduce it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 116A(2)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Electronic Record Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 116A(2)` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     `the generator of the electronic record is a non-party for section 116A(2)` e
              AND e's `that person did not generate, record or store it under the control of the party seeking to introduce it`
              AND NOT e's `evidence to the contrary is adduced`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 116A(3): unless evidence to the contrary is adduced, where the record was generated, recorded or stored by a
-- party adverse in interest to the party seeking to adduce it, the court is to presume that it is authentic in
-- relation to the authentication issues arising from its generation, recording or storage.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 116A(3)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Electronic Record Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 116A(3)` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     e's `the electronic record was generated, recorded or stored by a party adverse in interest to the party seeking to adduce it`
              AND NOT e's `evidence to the contrary is adduced`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 116A(5) and (6): the Minister may make regulations for an imaging process; where a record was recorded or
-- stored from a document produced pursuant to an approved process, the court is to presume, unless evidence to
-- the contrary is adduced, that the record accurately reproduces the document.  The regulations are not
-- encoded: whether the process was approved is a fact.  116A(7): the matters may be established by affidavit.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 116A(6)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Electronic Record Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 116A(6)` MEANS
    BRANCH IF     e's `the electronic record was recorded or stored from a document produced pursuant to an approved process`
              AND NOT e's `evidence to the contrary is adduced`
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `103 to 113. Burden of proof`

-- 103(1): whoever desires any court to give judgment as to any legal right or liability, dependent on the
-- existence of facts which the person asserts, must prove that those facts exist.  Illustration (a): A desires
-- judgment that B shall be punished for a crime; A must prove that B committed the crime.  103(2): the burden
-- lies on the person bound to prove.  104: the burden lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all
-- were given on either side.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 103(1) and 104"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden lies on the party who asserts the fact under section 104` IF
    b's `the party who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side is the party who asserts the fact`

-- 105: the burden of proof as to any particular fact lies on the person who wishes the court to believe in its
-- existence, unless it is provided by any law that the proof of that fact is to lie on a particular person.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 105"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden lies on the person who wishes the court to believe in the fact under section 105` IF
    NOT b's `a law provides that the proof of the fact is to lie on a particular person`

-- 106: the burden of proving any fact necessary to be proved to enable a person to give evidence of another
-- fact is on the person who wishes to give that evidence.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 106"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden of proving the preliminary fact lies on the person who wishes to give the evidence` IF
    b's `the fact is necessary to be proved in order to enable a person to give evidence of another fact`

-- 107: when a person is accused of an offence, the burden of proving the existence of circumstances bringing
-- the case within any of the general exceptions in the Penal Code 1871, or within any special exception or
-- proviso contained in any other part of the Penal Code 1871 or in any law defining the offence, is upon the
-- person, and the court is to presume the absence of such circumstances.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 107"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden of proving the circumstance lies on the accused under section 107` IF
        b's `the person is accused of an offence`
    AND (   b's `the circumstance brings the case within a general exception in the Penal Code 1871`
         OR b's `the circumstance brings the case within a special exception or proviso contained in another part of the Penal Code 1871`
         OR b's `the circumstance brings the case within a special exception or proviso contained in a law defining the offence`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 107"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of Presumption`
`the presumption under section 107` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the burden of proving the circumstance lies on the accused under section 107` b
           THEN JUST `the court is to presume`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 108: when any fact is especially within the knowledge of any person, the burden of proving that fact is upon
-- that person.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 108"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden lies on the person with special knowledge under section 108` IF
    b's `the fact is especially within the knowledge of a person`

-- 109: where it is shown that a person was alive within 30 years, the burden of proving that the person is
-- dead is on the person who affirms it.  110: where it is proved that the person has not been heard of for 7
-- years by those who would naturally have heard of the person if alive, the burden of proving that the person
-- is alive is "shifted" to the person who affirms it.  The two are applied as stated: 110 states a shift and so
-- governs where both are made out (NOTES.md, fork F7).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 109 and 110"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Person Bearing the Burden`
`the person bearing the burden on whether a person is alive` MEANS
    BRANCH IF b's `the question is whether a person is alive or dead and it is proved that the person has not been heard of for 7 years by those who would naturally have heard of the person if alive`
           THEN JUST `the person who affirms that the person is alive`
           IF b's `the question is whether a person is alive or dead and it is shown that the person was alive within 30 years`
           THEN JUST `the person who affirms that the person is dead`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 111"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden lies on the person who affirms that the relationship has not existed or has ceased under section 111` IF
    b's `the question is whether persons are partners, landlord and tenant, or principal and agent, and it has been shown that they have been acting as such`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 112"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden lies on the person who affirms that the person in possession is not the owner under section 112` IF
    b's `the question is whether a person is the owner of a thing of which the person is shown to be in possession`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 113"
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the burden of proving good faith lies on the party in a position of active confidence under section 113` IF
    b's `there is a question as to the good faith of a transaction between parties one of whom stands to the other in a position of active confidence`
